{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 13, :comments ["<b>And these you shall detest among the birds.</b> The word ״מִן״ implies a part of the whole, that is, the minority — because concerning birds our Sages of blessed memory said (Ḥullin 63b) that the clean species are more numerous than the unclean; therefore he enumerated the unclean. But among beasts the unclean are more numerous; therefore he enumerated the clean in the parashah of רְאֵה (Deuteronomy 14:4–5). And the reason for this is that the element of earth is thicker and coarser and produces greater clouding of the intellect than the element of air. And beasts have a larger share of the element of earth than of the element of air, and for this reason they are unable to fly in the air. Therefore the unclean among them are numerous, because of the abundance of earthiness in them, and by way of exception some clean ones are also found among them. And the opposite holds for birds: they have a larger share of the element of air, which is pure and refined, than of the element of earth; therefore the majority of them are clean. And by way of exception some unclean ones are found among them, and they are few. For the earth promotes materiality and the air promotes purity and spirituality. Therefore it says (Leviticus 20:25): ״וְהִבְדַּלְתֶּם בֵּין הַבְּהֵמָה הַטְּהֹרָה לַטְּמֵאָה וּבֵין הָעוֹף הַטָּמֵא לַטָּהֹר״ — he placed the clean beasts first and the unclean birds first, because whenever one separates a thing from its fellow, one separates the fewer from the many; and it is as though he said: you shall distinguish among beasts the clean from among the unclean, and the reverse among birds."], :book :leviticus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 11, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:36:15.209040290Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}